Z||000000||ryshask||05-07-2000||04:07 PM||||I see a lot of posts and information online about suspension mods, but I've not been able to find any comparisons. I have come accross a web page with new A arms and such, do they help. To what extent can the pre 88 fiero's be improved? <P>I have to replace one of the ball joints on this fiero next week. should I just go with the stock? <P>This car will receive a v8 transplant when the original v6 goes south.<P>I'm pretty much looking for the 'ultimate' fiero setup for suspension for pre 88 or should I just sell the 86 as soon as I get it and search for an 88? <P>Thanks for your time.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||05-07-2000||06:07 PM||||Remember the 88 suspension is different than the other years.  I've understood it was more difficult to modify the 88 than the other years.  But if you got the dough, you can do anything.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000002||Sillhouette.com||05-07-2000||06:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ryshask:<BR><B>I see a lot of posts and information online about suspension mods, but I've not been able to find any comparisons. I have come accross a web page with new A arms and such, do they help. To what extent can the pre 88 fiero's be improved? <P>I have to replace one of the ball joints on this fiero next week. should I just go with the stock? <P>This car will receive a v8 transplant when the original v6 goes south.<P>I'm pretty much looking for the 'ultimate' fiero setup for suspension for pre 88 or should I just sell the 86 as soon as I get it and search for an 88? <P>Thanks for your time.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well we (RSM race car) use suspension techniques lowering springs with KYB shocks all around, urethane busings everywhere and front and rear sway bars.  Cant beat that I think.  Also having R1 (hot)  makes a difference during cornering. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||05-07-2000||09:19 PM||||Polyurethane control arm and cradle bushings and ST springs are great if you don't mind having your teeth rattled out of your head, but the handling improvement and lower ride height are great.  I don't mind the trade off, and I drive mine every day. <P>Moog sells good, reasonably priced balljoints.  They make a slotted upper balljoint (Problem Solver) that allows front end camber adjustment, unlike the originals.  Cost me $20 a piece for the uppers, 30 for the front lowers, and 50 for the rears.  <P>JC Whitney has a reasonably priced rear anti-swaybar that works, but you'll need to buy the poly bushings for it separate.  <P>See the earlier bigger tire post for info there.<p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 05-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||05-07-2000||10:48 PM||||If you plan on going to a bigger more HP engine later, do all the bushings in poly now. I went all poly, KYB shocks and struts, Eibach Springs and KONI sway bars. The sway bar kits are $139 for front, $149 for rear but include all the bushings. Ride is deffinately STIFF, But oh so fun to drive!||||reg||1
